// kpp standard library: the allocation functions, here because they are global as C++'s are, as C's names are (--std-scope, design.md 16).

// The global allocation and deallocation functions ([new.delete.single], [new.delete.array]):
// `::operator new(n)` a block of n bytes (at least one), BadAlloc when malloc cannot give it, and
// `::operator delete(p)` gives it back; nothing for null. A program may replace any of them
// ([replacement.functions]; design.md, "Allocation functions"), and these call each other as C++'s
// do: new[] is new, a sized one is the unsized one, delete[] is delete. A new-expression calls them
// (or a class's own) only when the program declares its own; otherwise it allocates with malloc.
void* operator new(usize bytes) { return allocateBytes(bytes); }
void* operator new[](usize bytes) { return operator new(bytes); }
void operator delete(void* block) { free(block); }
void operator delete[](void* block) { operator delete(block); }
void operator delete(void* block, usize) { operator delete(block); }
void operator delete[](void* block, usize) { operator delete[](block); }
